lesson nature-new-plants-without-seeds v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=nature levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "New plants without seeds"
  goal "Tell a plant grown from a part of one plant from one grown from seed, and say why the first is a copy and the second may differ."

  look {
    scene 36x12 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        parts st of=runner
        narrowboat nb windows=3 pots=4
      }
    }
    say """
      On the narrowboat's roof Ada's best strawberry plant sends out runners: long stems with a small
      new plant at the end, which roots in the soil and can be cut free. A daffodil bulb splits into
      new bulbs, a willow stick pushed into the canal bank grows roots, and a sprouting potato grows a
      new plant. Each is a part of one plant, so the new plant is a copy of it. A seed comes from a
      flower, often with pollen from another plant, so a plant from seed may differ.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked nature.runner-part k=2 level=medium
    }
    show nature.runner-part k=0 hard-k=2
    show nature.seed-or-part k=0 hard-k=3
    show nature.copy-or-vary k=0 hard-k=2
    show nature.seed-or-part k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show nature.runner-part k=3 easy-k=1
    show nature.copy-or-vary k=1 hard-k=3
    level easy medium {
      show nature.seed-or-part k=3 easy-k=2
    }
    level hard {
      show nature.copies-disease k=0
      show nature.which-method k=0 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show nature.runner-count k=1 p=2 easy-k=0 easy-p=3 hard-k=2 hard-p=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show nature.which-method k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show nature.fewest-runners k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A plant grown from a part of one plant is a copy of it; a plant grown from seed may be different."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Plants make new plants in two ways. From seed, after pollination, which mixes two parents; and
    from a part of one plant, which makes a copy. The RHS ("How plants reproduce") lists "sending
    out runners – tiny new plants on long stems – as with strawberries", "bulbs dividing to form new
    small bulbs, called offsets", and cuttings, and says "new plants produced by cloning are
    identical to the parent plant", while "plants grown from seed may not be 'true to type'". That a
    potato grows from its tuber and a willow stick roots in wet ground is recalled; the runner counts
    in the story are a model, not one real bed. The step up from pollination and the seed test
    earlier this year is the second route and why it gives copies. The wrong idea to watch for is
    that every new plant starts as a seed, or that seeds from one plant are copies of it. In the
    child's working, look for "one plant or two" asked of each new plant. If the child is stuck,
    sort the six cases into two piles first, then stay here another day before the fewest runners.
    Off the page, root a cutting of mint or a spider plant's baby in a jar of water and watch for
    roots in a week or two. This meets England's year five programme of study: "describe the life
    process of reproduction in some plants and animals".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds the runner, the parts to find are the parent and the new plant, the new plants come from a runner, a packet of cress and a daffodil bulb, the copies are Ada's runner and Ben's seeds, the story runs one summer, the reason is to keep the sweet strawberries, and the fewest runners make twenty plants."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the parts to find are the runner and the new plant's roots, the new plants come from a willow stick, a potato and the towpath's sunflowers, the copies are the rose's cutting and its seeds, the core adds a bed of copies hit by a disease, the story finds the summer the plants pass a hundred, the reason is to find a new kind, and the fewest runners make sixty plants."
  }
}
